„­bible_version£KJV¤book§chapter¨sectionsš…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ1Moreover the word of the LORD came to me, saying,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÕGo and cry in the ears of Jerusalem, saying, Thus saith the LORD; I remember thee, the kindness of thy youth, the love of thine espousals, when thou wentest after me in the wilderness, in a land that was not sown.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”Israel was holiness unto the LORD, and the firstfruits of his increase: all that devour him shall offend; evil shall come upon them, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\Hear ye the word of the LORD, O house of Jacob, and all the families of the house of Israel:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”thus saith the LORD, What iniquity have your fathers found in me, that they are gone far from me, and have walked after vanity, and are become vain?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚNeither said they, Where is the LORD that brought us up out of the land of Egypt, that led us through the wilderness, through a land of deserts and of pits, through a land of drought, and of the shadow of death, through a land that no man passed through, and where no man dwelt?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ®And I brought you into a plentiful country, to eat the fruit thereof and the goodness thereof; but when ye entered, ye defiled my land, and made mine heritage an abomination.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÍThe priests said not, Where is the LORD? and they that handle the law knew me not: the pastors also transgressed against me, and the prophets prophesied by Baal, and walked after things that do not profit.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verse	ªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙdWherefore I will yet plead with you, saith the LORD, and with your children's children will I plead.¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|For pass over the isles of Chittim, and see; and send unto Kedar, and consider diligently, and see if there be such a thing.¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHath a nation changed their gods, which are yet no gods? but my people have changed their glory for that which doth not profit.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbBe astonished, O ye heavens, at this, and be horribly afraid, be ye very desolate, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢For my people have committed two evils; they have forsaken me the fountain of living waters, and hewed them out cisterns, broken cisterns, that can hold no water.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?Is Israel a servant? is he a homeborn slave? why is he spoiled?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtThe young lions roared upon him, and yelled, and they made his land waste: his cities are burned without inhabitant.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙJAlso the children of Noph and Tahapanes have broken the crown of thy head.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsHast thou not procured this unto thyself, in that thou hast forsaken the LORD thy God, when he led thee by the way?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ And now what hast thou to do in the way of Egypt, to drink the waters of Sihor? or what hast thou to do in the way of Assyria, to drink the waters of the river?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙõThine own wickedness shall correct thee, and thy backslidings shall reprove thee: know therefore and see that it is an evil thing and bitter, that thou hast forsaken the LORD thy God, and that my fear is not in thee, saith the Lord GOD of hosts.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½For of old time I have broken thy yoke, and burst thy bands; and thou saidst, I will not transgress; when upon every high hill and under every green tree thou wanderest, playing the harlot.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡Yet I had planted thee a noble vine, wholly a right seed: how then art thou turned into the degenerate plant of a strange vine unto me?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzFor though thou wash thee with nitre, and take thee much soap, yet thine iniquity is marked before me, saith the Lord GOD.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ©How canst thou say, I am not polluted, I have not gone after Baalim? see thy way in the valley, know what thou hast done: thou art a swift dromedary traversing her ways;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÈa wild ass used to the wilderness, that snuffeth up the wind at her pleasure; in her occasion who can turn her away? all they that seek her will not weary themselves; in her month they shall find her.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWithhold thy foot from being unshod, and thy throat from thirst: but thou saidst, There is no hope: no; for I have loved strangers, and after them will I go.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ•As the thief is ashamed when he is found, so is the house of Israel ashamed; they, their kings, their princes, and their priests, and their prophets,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÓsaying to a stock, Thou art my father; and to a stone, Thou hast brought me forth: for they have turned their back unto me, and not their face: but in the time of their trouble they will say, Arise, and save us.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ³But where are thy gods that thou hast made thee? let them arise, if they can save thee in the time of thy trouble: for according to the number of thy cities are thy gods, O Judah.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUWherefore will ye plead with me? ye all have transgressed against me, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ†In vain have I smitten your children; they received no correction: your own sword hath devoured your prophets, like a destroying lion.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ«O generation, see ye the word of the LORD. Have I been a wilderness unto Israel? a land of darkness? wherefore say my people, We are lords; we will come no more unto thee?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlCan a maid forget her ornaments, or a bride her attire? yet my people have forgotten me days without number.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse#¦verses“„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaWhy trimmest thou thy way to seek love? therefore hast thou also taught the wicked ones thy ways.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒAlso in thy skirts is found the blood of the souls of the poor innocents: I have not found it by secret search, but upon all these.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”Yet thou sayest, Because I am innocent, surely his anger shall turn from me. Behold, I will plead with thee, because thou sayest, I have not sinned.¦layout ªlast_verse#…¤book§chapter«first_verse$ªlast_verse%¦verses’„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwWhy gaddest thou about so much to change thy way? thou also shalt be ashamed of Egypt, as thou wast ashamed of Assyria.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“Yea, thou shalt go forth from him, and thine hands upon thine head: for the LORD hath rejected thy confidences, and thou shalt not prosper in them.¦layout ªlast_verse%